    "narrative": "The offensive focus for Ghost Ride the WHIP today rests squarely on **Brice Turang**, who anchors our lineup with a 63.99 efficiency score. The matchup against Roki Sasaki is analytically fascinating; while the raw SP skill of Sasaki is formidable, **Brice Turang** benefits from a high-leverage combination of a move to the #2 spot and a distinct speed advantage. His score is bolstered by a +4% adjustment for Sasaki\u2019s deliberate delivery, which signals a massive green light for our primary stolen base engine. Conversely, **Jose Caballero** represents our most precarious start at a 40.84 score. Facing Logan Gilbert, he suffers from a significant -14% SP skill penalty and a -13% platoon drag, forcing him to the bottom of the order where his production floor is dangerously thin.\n\nIn our bench considerations, **Josh Naylor** is the primary player to watch if lineups shift. While he currently slots in at 54.38, he remains on the bubble; his performance against Max Fried is a classic high-risk, high-reward play where his platoon disadvantage (-5%) is partially offset by positive park factors. We are prioritizing the stolen base efficiency of **Brice Turang** and the order-derived value of **Cole Young** (50.48) over pure power outputs today, as we attempt to close the gap in the speed category while navigating a difficult slate of opposing arms like Gilbert and Fried. We are fortunate that the injury report remains static, though we continue to monitor the absence of **Corbin Carroll**, **James Wood**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, and **Tyler Soderstrom**, whose collective unavailability severely restricts our tactical flexibility. Managers should be prepared for late-inning pivots if confirmed lineups drop **Zach McKinstry** from the #9 spot, which would necessitate a reassessment of our bottom-tier production strategy.",
